th1-surjection-curation-001+math1-bijection-curation-001+math1-union-curation-001+math1-proper-curation-001+math1-complement-curation-001+math1-complement-render-001+math1-division-curation-001+math1-reciprocal-curation-001+math1-inequality-curation-001+math1-inequality-feedback-001+math1-maximum-curation-001+math1-minimum-curation-001+math1-boundedset-curation-001+math1-endpoint-curation-001+audit-groups-20261027+topup-math-i-20261028+topup-math-ii-20261028+topup-pt-20261028+topup-math-iii-20261028","concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/taylor-polynomial.html","concept":{"concept_id":"Year-1/2a-Math-III-Analysis/taylor-polynomial.html","questions":[{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_001","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What is the $k$-th order Taylor polynomial of $f$ at $x = a$?","options":["$P_k(x) = f(x)+f'(x)(a-x)+\\cdots+\\dfrac{f^{(k)}(x)}{k!}a^k$","$P_k(x) = \\sum_{n = 0}^{\\infty}\\dfrac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!}(x-a)^n$","$P_k(x) = f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)+\\cdots+\\dfrac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!}(x-a)^k$","$P_k(x) = f(a)(x-a)^k$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f825282472452c33645474e8","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_002","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"conceptual","question":"What does the $k$-th order Taylor polynomial capture from the Taylor series, when that series exists?","options":["Only the constant term","The initial $k+1$ terms centred at the same point","Only terms of degree higher than $k$","The entire infinite series for every smooth function"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"cc25991817b10924e3de639e","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_004","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the $3$rd order Taylor polynomial of $\\sin x$ around $x = 0$?","options":["$P_3(x) = x-\\dfrac{x^3}{3!}$","$P_3(x) = 1+x+\\dfrac{x^2}{2!}$","$P_3(x) = x+\\dfrac{x^3}{3!}$","$P_3(x) = \\sin x$ exactly for all $x$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"c25d9c46d4c56b56f1d887b7","time_limit":25},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_005","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which key property does $P_k$ share with $f$ at $x = a$?","options":["They have the same derivatives of all orders at each point of the domain.","They have the same derivatives up to the $k$-th order at $a$.","They are always equal on the whole domain.","They have no derivatives at $a$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"98cae7cc15fd760549d3239b","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_006","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"Which distinction between Taylor polynomials and Taylor series is stated?","options":["A Taylor polynomial can be written only for real analytic functions, not for merely smooth ones.","A Taylor series always converges to any smooth function.","A Taylor polynomial never uses derivatives.","A Taylor polynomial uses finitely many derivatives; the full series equals $f$ only when $f$ is analytic."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4e7d77c6b67858b5fc8b56be","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_007","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Suppose $f(2) = 3$, $f'(2) = -1$, and $f''(2) = 4$. What is $P_2(x)$ at $a = 2$?","options":["$3-2(x-2)+4(x-2)^2$","$3-(x-2)+4(x-2)^2$","$3-(x-2)+2(x-2)^2$","$3+4(x-2)-\\dfrac{1}{2}(x-2)^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"f048a72cbc0183f14bf3118d","time_limit":30},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_015","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the second-order Taylor polynomial of $\\mathrm{e}^{2x^2}$ at $x=0$?","options":["$1+2x^2+2x^4$","$1+4x^2$","$1+2x^2$","$1+x^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"9df53e3b66d8b0658014d275","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_016","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"What is the first-order Taylor polynomial of $f$ at $a$?","options":["$f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)$","$f'(a)+f(a)(x-a)$","$f(a)(x-a)$","$f''(a)(x-a)^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"d4d07a29c2920bdf9433cc07","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_017","difficulty":"basic","question_type":"application","question":"For $f(x)=x^2$ at $a=1$, what is $P_1(x)$?","options":["$x^2$","$1+2(x-1)$","$2+x^2$","$1+(x-1)^2$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"4beffc7edebe1abaabccc39f","time_limit":20},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_008_curated20261027_001","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"conceptual","question":"For $f(x)=1/x$ at $a=1$ the Taylor polynomials are $P_k(x)=\\sum_{j=0}^{k}(-1)^j(x-1)^j$. What happens to $P_k(3)$ as the order $k$ grows?","options":["It moves further away from $f(3)=\\tfrac{1}{3}$: the terms $(-2)^j$ grow in size, so the approximation is only guaranteed near $a=1$.","It converges to $f(3)=\\tfrac{1}{3}$, because a higher order always gives a better approximation.","It equals $f(3)=\\tfrac{1}{3}$ exactly from $k=2$ on, since the polynomial then matches $f$ and its first two derivatives.","It stays at $P_1(3)=-1$, because the terms of degree $2$ and higher vanish at $x=3$."],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"11eb776e9f55c60a089a5597","time_limit":50},{"id":"year_1_2a_math_iii_analysis_taylor_polynomial_f7861fea_012_curated20261027_002","difficulty":"standard","question_type":"application","question":"Let $f$ be twice differentiable at $a$ and let $Q(x)=f(a)+f'(a)(x-a)+f''(a)(x-a)^2$, i.e. the second-order Taylor polynomial but without the factor $\\tfrac{1}{2!}$. What is $Q''(a)$?","options":["$2f''(a)$","$f''(a)$","$\\tfrac{1}{2}f''(a)$","$0$"],"answer_mode":"server","feedback_revision":"56de2699cc6a6e9eb50e7b24","time_limit":30}]}}